window.NMR_EXPERIMENT = Object.freeze({"meta":{"number":"A.1","slug":"a-1","kind":"reference","title":"Answers","titleZh":"习题答案","pages":[{"id":"pdf-283","pdfPage":283,"bookPage":273,"en":"content/pages/pdf-283.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-283.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-284","pdfPage":284,"bookPage":274,"en":"content/pages/pdf-284.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-284.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-285","pdfPage":285,"bookPage":275,"en":"content/pages/pdf-285.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-285.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-286","pdfPage":286,"bookPage":276,"en":"content/pages/pdf-286.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-286.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-287","pdfPage":287,"bookPage":277,"en":"content/pages/pdf-287.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-287.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-288","pdfPage":288,"bookPage":278,"en":"content/pages/pdf-288.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-288.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-289","pdfPage":289,"bookPage":279,"en":"content/pages/pdf-289.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-289.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-290","pdfPage":290,"bookPage":280,"en":"content/pages/pdf-290.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-290.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-291","pdfPage":291,"bookPage":281,"en":"content/pages/pdf-291.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-291.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-292","pdfPage":292,"bookPage":282,"en":"content/pages/pdf-292.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-292.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-293","pdfPage":293,"bookPage":283,"en":"content/pages/pdf-293.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-293.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-294","pdfPage":294,"bookPage":284,"en":"content/pages/pdf-294.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-294.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-295","pdfPage":295,"bookPage":285,"en":"content/pages/pdf-295.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-295.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-296","pdfPage":296,"bookPage":286,"en":"content/pages/pdf-296.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-296.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-297","pdfPage":297,"bookPage":287,"en":"content/pages/pdf-297.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-297.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-298","pdfPage":298,"bookPage":288,"en":"content/pages/pdf-298.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-298.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-299","pdfPage":299,"bookPage":289,"en":"content/pages/pdf-299.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-299.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-300","pdfPage":300,"bookPage":290,"en":"content/pages/pdf-300.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-300.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-301","pdfPage":301,"bookPage":291,"en":"content/pages/pdf-301.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-301.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-302","pdfPage":302,"bookPage":292,"en":"content/pages/pdf-302.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-302.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-303","pdfPage":303,"bookPage":293,"en":"content/pages/pdf-303.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-303.zh.html"},{"id":"pdf-304","pdfPage":304,"bookPage":294,"en":"content/pages/pdf-304.en.html","zh":"content/pages/pdf-304.zh.html"}],"chapter":"Appendix"},"english":{"pdf-283":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>Answers Chapter 1</h3><h4>1.1 <sup>1</sup>H NMR</h4>\r\n<p>A. H-4 has the longest relaxation time.</p><p>B. ABCD.</p><p>C. Roof effect.</p>\r\n<p>D. This is governed by the Karplus equation, which predicts a very small vicinal spin coupling when the dihedral angle is close to 90°.</p>\r\n<h4>1.2 APT-<sup>13</sup>C-NMR</h4>\r\n<p>A. R. R. Ernst published a calculation of the optimum pulse angle as a function of repetition time and relaxation time in an FT NMR experiment. This Ernst angle gives the optimum signal-to-noise ratio in a given time under these boundary conditions. See R. R. Ernst, W. A. Anderson, “Application of Fourier Transform Spectroscopy to Magnetic Resonance,” <i>Rev. Sci. Instr.</i> 1966, 37, 93–102.</p>\r\n<p>B. This is the <sup>1</sup><i>J</i><sub>CH</sub> spin-coupling constant. A compromise value of 145 Hz is usually chosen.</p>\r\n<p>C. Composite-pulse decoupling is a train of r.f. pulses of different lengths and phases in a defined “rhythm.” One such scheme is WALTZ-16; see A. J. Shaka, J. Keeler, T. Frenkiel, R. Freeman, “An improved sequence for broadband decoupling: WALTZ-16,” <i>J. Magn. Reson.</i> 1983, 52, 335–338.</p>\r\n<p>D. Deuterium has spin <i>I</i> = 1; the carbon signal therefore splits into three lines corresponding to <i>I</i><sub>z</sub> = −1, 0, +1 of the deuterium spin, with an approximately 1:1:1 ratio. The <i>J</i><sub>CH</sub> coupling can be calculated using the ratio of <i>J</i><sub>D</sub> and <i>J</i><sub>H</sub>.</p>\r\n<p>E. The carbonyl carbon at <i>δ</i><sub>C</sub> = 169.3 ppm and C-12 at <i>δ</i><sub>C</sub> = 77.6 ppm.</p>\r\n<h4>1.3 COSY</h4>\r\n<p>A. A typical COSY on a 400 MHz instrument must sample a 10 ppm proton chemical-shift range, corresponding to 4 kHz. The <i>t</i><sub>1</sub> increment is therefore 1/(4000 Hz) = 250 μs; 256 increments give a maximum <i>t</i><sub>1</sub> of 64 ms. The best COSY transfer from <span class=\"inline-equation\">2<i>I</i><sub>1x</sub><i>I</i><sub>2z</sub></span> to <span class=\"inline-equation\">2<i>I</i><sub>1z</sub><i>I</i><sub>2y</sub></span> occurs when sin π<i>Jt</i><sub>1</sub> = 1, i.e. <i>Jt</i><sub>1</sub> = 0.5. For <i>J</i> = 7 Hz, 7 Hz × 0.064 s ≈ 0.45, giving efficient transfer. For a small coupling of about 1 Hz, the transfer is inefficient (1 Hz × 0.064 s ≈ 0.06). A fixed delay before and after the mixing pulse starts the transfer at a larger effective <i>t</i><sub>1</sub>.</p>\r\n<p>B. Digital resolution in the indirect dimension F<sub>1</sub> is always limited. Spin-coupling values should therefore be extracted only from the direct dimension F<sub>2</sub>.</p>\r\n<p>C. Creation of double-quantum magnetization requires evolved anti-phase magnetization <span class=\"inline-equation\">2<i>I</i><sub>1x</sub><i>I</i><sub>2z</sub></span> to be transferred into <span class=\"inline-equation\">2<i>I</i><sub>1x</sub><i>I</i><sub>2y</sub></span>.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-284":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>This process is efficient only at higher <i>t</i><sub>1</sub> values, not at the beginning (see A).</p>\r\n<p>D. The double-quantum filter suppresses singlets and reduces more complex spin systems to the AX case. All cross peaks in a DQF COSY therefore have the AX pattern.</p>\r\n<p>E. The Karplus equation determines vicinal spin coupling. One proton of C-15 has a dihedral angle close to 90° relative to the C16–H16 vector.</p>\r\n<h4>1.4 NOESY</h4>\r\n<p>A. Pulse p3 places the spins along −z in the rotating frame. They then relax toward equilibrium magnetization with time constant T<sub>1</sub>. Depending on chemical shift, several spins are already in the positive regime and need only one or two T<sub>1</sub> periods to reach equilibrium. The best mixing time is therefore around one T<sub>1</sub>, allowing effective cross relaxation.</p>\r\n<p>B. Build-up curves are measured by recording several NOESY spectra with different mixing times. The volume integrals of the cross peaks are plotted against mixing time; a linear fit to the first points is called the initial-rate approximation.</p>\r\n<p>C. Only in the initial linear part of a build-up curve can a two-spin pair interaction related to distance be assumed. At longer mixing times other spins contribute, and spin diffusion may reduce the integrals. At very long mixing times most spins have reached equilibrium and no longer give cross-relaxation values.</p>\r\n<p>D. NOESY cross relaxation starts from −z (population inversion) and continues until the spins reach +z equilibrium magnetization. ROESY starts from zero (equal populations) and therefore has only half the dynamic range.</p>\r\n<p>E. Diastereotopic methylene groups or ortho aromatic protons, for which the distance can be calculated geometrically. Care is required if other protons are nearby.</p>\r\n<h4>1.5 HSQC</h4>\r\n<p>A. HMQC uses only four r.f. pulses, whereas HSQC uses ten and is therefore more sensitive to misadjustment. During <i>t</i><sub>1</sub> in HMQC, H,H coupling also evolves because the proton spins are transverse. This broadens lines in F<sub>1</sub> and reduces resolution in crowded regions.</p>\r\n<p>B. Product-operator treatment shows that for CH<sub>2</sub> groups the double back transfer cancels the sensitivity gain observed for CH groups.</p>\r\n<p>A methylene group is described by two proton spins, <i>I</i><sub>H1</sub> and <i>I</i><sub>H2</sub>, and one carbon spin, <i>I</i><sub>C</sub>. In the following calculation, terms containing cos π<i>J</i><sub>CH</sub>d5 = 0 are omitted and only terms containing sin π<i>J</i><sub>CH</sub>d5 = 1 are retained.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-285":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>After the INEPT transfer the relevant terms are <span class=\"inline-equation\">−2<i>I</i><sub>H1z</sub><i>I</i><sub>Cy</sub></span> and <span class=\"inline-equation\">−2<i>I</i><sub>H2z</sub><i>I</i><sub>Cy</sub></span>. Both develop carbon chemical shift during <i>t</i><sub>1</sub> as follows:</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-1\"><img alt=\"Product-operator equations, group 1, from the HSQC answer.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-1.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>Each of these terms enters the double INEPT back transfer. The first two INEPT pulses p16 and p7 convert these terms into</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-2\"><img alt=\"Product-operator equations, group 2, from the HSQC answer.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-2.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>Evolution of <i>J</i><sub>CH</sub> now leads, because this is a three-spin system, to more complex terms:</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-3\"><img alt=\"Product-operator equations, group 3, from the HSQC answer.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-3.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>Pulses p18 and p9 convert these terms into</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-4\"><img alt=\"Product-operator equations, group 4, from the HSQC answer.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-4.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>The subsequent <i>J</i><sub>CH</sub> evolution in the second spin-echo period leads to</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-5\"><img alt=\"Product-operator equations, group 5, from the HSQC answer.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-5.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>All observable terms containing a sin Ω<sub>C</sub><i>t</i> modulation also have a cos π<i>J</i><sub>CH</sub>d5 = 0 component and therefore cancel. The final pulse p11 creates the observable magnetization:</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-6\"><img alt=\"Product-operator equations, group 6, from the HSQC answer.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-6.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>This is in contrast to the result for a CH group. Thus, a sensitivity enhancement or preservation of equivalent pathways will not function in this case. A common remedy is to set delay d5 not to 1/4<i>J</i><sub>CH</sub>, but to 1/8<i>J</i><sub>CH</sub>; this compromise helps the methyl and methylene signals.</p>\r\n<p>C. The best water suppression is usually achieved by using the pathway via the heteronucleus; therefore no additional water-suppression scheme is usually needed.</p>\r\n<p>D. HSQC stands for <strong>Heteronuclear Single Quantum Coherence</strong>. In this context it means that terms such as <span class=\"inline-equation\">−2<i>I</i><sub>Hy</sub><i>I</i><sub>Cz</sub></span> are present during <i>t</i><sub>1</sub>; only one spin species, namely <sup>13</sup>C, is transverse, in contrast to HMQC.</p>\r\n<p>E. 90° pulses have a much better frequency-excitation range than twice-as-long 180° pulses.</p>\r\n<p>F. In principle, if the molecule contains a chiral center, all methylene groups are diastereotopic. However, the degree of diastereotopicity depends on the distance from the stereogenic center.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-286":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>1.6 HMBC</h4>\r\n<p>A. The delay between p1 and p5 is much longer than the corresponding delay in HSQC; therefore relaxation effects may be crucial. Since <sup>13</sup>C decoupling is not used, all correlation signals are in principle doublets rather than sharp singlets. Transfer from protons to carbons is optimal for only a fraction of the signals.</p>\r\n<p>B. 125 to 170 Hz, not taking sp-hybridized carbon atoms into account.</p>\r\n<p>C. The spin-coupling constant of C-4 is especially large (168 Hz) because of the ortho nitrogen substituent. Similarly, C-17 has a low spin-coupling constant (133 Hz) because of its fully aliphatic environment.</p>\r\n<p>D. The final carbon pulse p7 creates anti-phase magnetization, which during acquisition develops into in-phase magnetization, but with different spin-coupling constants. In F<sub>1</sub> there is also modulation by H,H spin coupling. All these factors lead to phase distortions.</p>\r\n<p>E. The first carbon pulse p3 is applied after a delay optimized near the low end of the coupling-constant range, and the second carbon pulse p4 follows after a delay optimized near the high end. The long-range spin couplings are not significantly affected by these two pulses.</p>\r\n<p>The sum of the effects of gradients g1 to g3 is zero. Thus, after g3 only signal contributions that retained the coherence level present at the onset of g1 are refocused; these are the long-range coherences. The <sup>1</sup><i>J</i> coherences change coherence level because of p3 and p4 and are therefore further dephased by g2 and g3.</p>\r\n<p>F. Before gradient g4 the coherence level is <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup><i>I</i><sub>N</sub><sup>+</sup></span>, which p6 changes into <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup><i>I</i><sub>N</sub><sup>−</sup></span>. If the proton-to-nitrogen gyromagnetic-ratio ratio is 10:1, the former corresponds to −9 and the latter to −11. A gradient strength of 80% for g4 dephases <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup><i>I</i><sub>N</sub><sup>+</sup></span> by 80 × (−9) = −720 units. Accordingly, g5 must be −720/−11 = 65.45% to pass only <sup>1</sup>H–<sup>15</sup>N correlations. To select first <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup><i>I</i><sub>N</sub><sup>−</sup></span> and then <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup><i>I</i><sub>N</sub><sup>+</sup></span> in echo/anti-echo mode, set g4 first to −65.45% and g5 to 80%.</p>\r\n<p>G. On current high-field spectrometers the spectral width for <sup>13</sup>C exceeds 20 kHz. A usual hard 180° pulse is insufficient to refocus carbon signals at both ends of the spectral range. Frequency-swept pulses are therefore used (see Chapter 8.4, “Chirped Pulses”).</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-287":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>Answers Chapter 2</h3>\r\n<h4>2.1 2D J-Resolved 1H NMR Spectroscopy</h4>\r\n<p>A. Due to the spin coupling with deuterium the proton in CHD2CD3S=O displays a quintuplet with the intensities 1 : 2: 3: 2: 1 . For deuterium with spin I = 1, each deuterium nucleus holds three magnetic spin numbers mz= −1, 0, +1, which combine in five overall spin numbers of −2, −1, 0, +1, +2. In the 2D J-resolved spectrum this signal remains unchanged at the center line in F1, since this is a heteronuclear spin coupling.</p>\r\n<p>B. Consider two protons with different chemicals shifts and coupled to each other, which form in the normal 1D NMR spectrum an AX spin system. After excitation by p1, chemical shift and spin coupling evolve during t1. The chemical shifts are refocused by the 180° pulse p2. At the end of t1 we have in terms of the product operator formalism considering only spin 1:</p>\r\n<p class=\"display-equation\"><span class=\"inline-equation\">−2<i>I</i><sub>1x</sub><i>I</i><sub>2z</sub> sin(2π<i>J</i><sub>12</sub><i>t</i><sub>1</sub>) + <i>I</i><sub>1y</sub> cos(2π<i>J</i><sub>12</sub><i>t</i><sub>1</sub>)</span></p>\r\n<p>In the subsequent acquisition all signals will thus be modulated with the sine and the cosine of the spin coupling constants at the same time, i.e. overlaying absorptive and dispersive line shapes, which yields a twisted shape.</p>\r\n<p>C. This is the usual EXORCYCLE phase scheme, which alleviates pulse imperfections, i.e. too short or too long pulses compared with the nominal ones.</p>\r\n<p>D. The spin echo sequence removes at the point of the spin echo the chemical shift contribution and analogically also the inhomogeneity of the magnetic field causing line broadening. The multiplets in F1 therefore display their natural line widths.</p>\r\n<h4>2.2 ROESY</h4>\r\n<p>A. Consider a spin in originally –y direction. Pulse p2 will move it to the +y axis, whereas pulse p3 will turn it back to the –y axis. Thus the phase cycle of the spin lock always reorients the spins in the xy plane. This prevents a rapid dephasing and reduces errors of the 180° pulses.</p>\r\n<p>B. In ROESY only the mixing of the weak relaxation terms should occur. TOCSY mixing requires stronger mixing pulses, since spin coupling terms have to be considered.</p>\r\n<p>C. H-8 and H-13 are stereochemically in opposite directions, thus the cross relaxation will be very low, but the spin coupling rather large.</p>\r\n<p>D. The signal of H-1 has a cross relaxation with H-17 and H-16. Only H-1, but not H-4 are within the reach of these spins.</p>\r\n<h4>2.3 TOCSY</h4>\r\n<p>A. Spin lock: <i>I</i><sub>0</sub> = 100 W; 10 log(<i>I</i><sub>0</sub>/<i>I</i>) = 12.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-288":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>log (<i>I</i><sub>0</sub>/<i>I</i>) = 1.2; <i>I</i><sub>0</sub>/<i>I</i> = 15.84, <i>I</i> = 6.31 W; 6.31 W × 0.3 s = 1.893 W s.</p>\r\n<p>Hard pulse: 100 W × 10 × 10<sup>−6</sup> s = 10<sup>−3</sup> W s.</p>\r\n<p>One spin-lock pulse lasting 300 ms dissipates about 2000 times the energy of a single hard pulse.</p>\r\n<p>B. The TOCSY transfer leads to phase distortions in the cross peaks; therefore magnitude processing is advisable.</p>\r\n<p>C. Different spin-lock schemes have different effectiveness with respect to the spectral width covered. For the MLEV16 spin lock the factor 1.7 has been reported, meaning that instead of, e.g., 4000 Hz one needs to calculate 4000 × 1.7 = 6800 Hz. The equation <i>p</i><sub>1</sub> = 1/(4 × 6800 s<sup>−1</sup>) then gives 36.8 μs for the individual 90° pulse length.</p>\r\n<p>D.</p><dl class=\"appendix-definitions\"><dt>MLEV</dt><dd>Malcom Levitt</dd><dt>DIPSI</dt><dd>Decoupling in the presence of scalar interactions</dd><dt>GARP</dt><dd>Globally alternating rectangular pulses</dd></dl>\r\n<h4>2.4 HSQC-TOCSY</h4><p>A.</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-288-hsqc-tocsy-pathway\"><img alt=\"Product-operator pathway for the HSQC-TOCSY answer.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-288-hsqc-tocsy-pathway.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>B. Before each of the trim pulses p3 and p6 the proton magnetization is in the x-direction; thus the desired magnetization is not affected if the trim pulses act from the same direction.</p>\r\n<p>C. The same argument as for question B holds. Directly before the onset of the spin lock, the proton magnetization is in the y-direction, and therefore the spin lock will catch it.</p>\r\n<p>D. This is due to the well-known anomeric effect, an interaction of the lone pairs of the electrons at the oxygen atoms. According to the Karplus equation one expects for this configuration only a small coupling constant, since H-1′ and H-2′ are in equatorial/axial positions relative to each other.</p>\r\n<h4>2.5 HOESY</h4>\r\n<p>A. <sup>6</sup>Li has a very long relaxation time; the proton relaxation time is considerably shorter. During the mixing time d3, when cross relaxation occurs, the protons would probably be fully relaxed before communication with <sup>6</sup>Li is successful.</p>\r\n<p>B. Carbon atoms bearing one or more hydrogen atoms will not need cross relaxation to distant protons; thus the experiment is only of value for quaternary carbon atoms. Structural questions for the former are usually better addressed by HMBC or related methods.</p>\r\n<p>C. The gradient ratio g1:g3 must equal the ratio of the NMR frequencies of these nuclei.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-289":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>2.6 INADEQUATE</h4>\r\n<p>A. In natural abundance, the occurance of 13C,13C pairs in the same molecule is 1.1 % × 1.1 %, thus INADEQUATE can only detect 10−4 of the concentration given.</p>\r\n<p>B. Since we are measuring 13C, the spectral distance between spincoupled carbon atoms can be up to 200 ppm, which, e.g., on a 600 MHz spectrometer corresponds to 30 KHz. A normal hard 180° pulse does not cover this frequency difference. See chapter 8.4.</p>\r\n<p>C. At the time of g1 double-quantum coherence is present, thus the effect of g1 is doubled. At the time of g2 single-quantum coherence is present, thus the strength of g2 must be twice that of g1 to achieve refocusing.</p>\r\n<p>D.</p>\r\n<h4>2.7 ADEQUATE</h4>\r\n<p>A. INADEQUATE detects 10<sup>−4</sup> of the concentration with carbon sensitivity; ADEQUATE detects the same amount with proton sensitivity. According to R. R. Ernst, G. Bodenhausen, and A. Wokaun, <i>Principles of NMR in One and Two Dimensions</i> (Clarendon Press, Oxford, 1987), p. 468, the sensitivity of a 2D experiment is proportional to <span class=\"inline-equation\">γ<sub>exc</sub>γ<sub>obs</sub><sup>3/2</sup></span>. For INADEQUATE both γ values refer to carbon, giving 1 × 1<sup>3/2</sup> = 1. For ADEQUATE both refer to proton, giving 4 × 4<sup>3/2</sup> = 32.</p>\r\n<p>B. 104, because the possibility of a 13C-13C pair at natural abundance is (1.1*10−2)*(1.1*10−2 ) and all other signals must be suppressed.</p>\r\n<p>C. H-23a and H-23b are bound to the same carbon atom with GC = 64.6; C-22 resonates at GC = 127.34 which gives an ADEQUA- TE value of 191.94. H-20a and H-20b are bound to a carbon with GC = 52.68; C-21 has a GC = 140.45 which adds to 193.13. These numbers are not distinguished at this resolution.</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure source-figure-wide\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-289-fig-1\"><img alt=\"Figure from source PDF page 289.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/spectra/pdf-289-fig-1.webp\"/></figure>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside class=\"source-sidebar\">\r\n<div class=\"source-sidebar-label\">Source sidebar</div>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-289-vector-auto-1\"><img alt=\"Figure from source PDF page 289.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/chemical-structures/pdf-289-vector-auto-1.webp\"/></figure>\r\n</aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-290":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>2.8 J-HMBC</h4>\r\n<p>A. Although this is a 2JCH spin coupling, a dihedral angle including the oxygen atom with its free electron pairs will become important and influences this spin coupling.</p>\r\n<p>B. According to a molecular model (see http://csi.chemie.tu-darmstadt.de/ak/immel/structures-nmr.html) the dihedral angle between C-10 and H-12 is −113°; therefore the 3JCH should be very small according to the Karplus equation.</p>\r\n<p>C. In \"constant time\" pulse sequences the same delay always exists between the first excitation and the start of detection, whereas in \"normal\" pulse sequences this delay is incremented from short to long values. Due to relaxation in the constant time one has to face signal loss.</p>\r\n<p>D. In that case there is an additional modulation in F1 by the proton chemical shifts, and the JCH doublets have an offset with respect to each other.</p>\r\n<h4>2.9 Gated Decoupling</h4>\r\n<p>A. The broad split doublet stems from C-12 which has long range CH spin couplings from H-13, H-11, and H-23, The sharp doublet stems from the residual CHCl3 signal.</p>\r\n<p>B. The protons of C-17 have the same relative chemical shift. The signal of C-17 splits therefore into a triplet with an additional long range triplet caused by the protons of C-18. In contrast, the protons of C-11 display a high degree of diastereotopicity and cause distinctly different 1JCH spin coupling with C-11. Therefore we observe a doublet of doublets.</p>\r\n<p>C. C-3 is fixed in the aromatic ring and shows the 1JCH to its own proton H-3, a 3JCH to H-1 and a further small splitting, probably to H-4. C-22 is surrounded by five protons with a similar 3JCH; furthermore, this part of the molecule may have some flexibility.</p>\r\n<p>D. From the HMBC spectrum it can be seen that the C=O atom couples with both H-11a/b and H-12.We find the best agreement using the following proton parameters: GH-11a = 3.132, GH-11b = 2.670, GH-12 = 4.288, 2JH-11a, H-11b = −17.34 Hz, 3JH-11a, H-12 = 3.34 Hz and 3JH-11b, H-12 = 8.47 Hz. With these as proton basis and the chemical shift of the C=O atom at 169.2 we used 2JC,H-11a = 8 Hz, 2JC,H-11b = 6.3, and 3JC,H-12 = 1.9 Hz to give perfect agreement.</p>\r\n<p>E. The build-up and the decay of the NOE effect follow the same time constant, and therefore it may be best to keep these two times equal.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-291":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>Answers Chapter 3</h3>\r\n<h4>3.1 Water suppression by Presaturation</h4>\r\n<p>A. Water, with the molecular weight of 18, is 55.55 molar in itself, and due to the two protons it would yield a relative NMR signal of 111.11 units and, because of the mixture of 90 % H2O/10 % D2O, a relative signal of 100 units. A 2 mmol solution of sucrose in this mixture will therefore have a relative integral for 1 proton of 2*10−5, which is 50000 times less than the integral of the water.</p>\r\n<p>B. No, because of saturation transfer to the NH protons.</p>\r\n<p>C. No, because cw irradiation works only for one frequency. There exist some techniques to cycle through a set of frequencies during the presaturation delay for multiple saturation.</p>\r\n<p>D. AA' BB' CC'</p>\r\n<h4>3.2 Solvent Suppression by 1D-NOESY</h4>\r\n<p>A. The first acquisition of the 1D-NOESY is essentially a 270° pulse, i.e., 90°x, 90°x, 90°x with delays between pulses. The second step of the acquisition is 90°–x, 90°x, 90°x, and this is identical to the first scheme except for the –x phase of the first pulse. The result, however, is only a 90°x pulse for any resonance experiencing the full intended pulse effects. The receiver subtracts the second from the first scan. Since resonances outside the center of the coil react differently to the first scan and the second, this leads finally to a volume restriction, i.e. the cancellation of signals stemming from the volume ends of the sample with inhomogeneous B0 field, see ref.[4].</p>\r\n<p>B. Yes, this will help. A long T1 of the solvent will prohibit its full recovery after p2.</p>\r\n<p>C. At both points in time the magnetization should be in the z-direction. Z-gradients destroy spurious xy-magnetization left over from previous scans (\" spoiling gradient\").</p>\r\n<h4>3.3 Water Suppression by SOGGY Excitation Sculpting</h4>\r\n<p>A. A 90° pulse would then be 1 ms long. The intensity of this pulse, expressed in frequency units, is JB1/2S = 1/(4p90) = 1/0.004 = 250 Hz, and this is, roughly speaking, its excitation bandwidth. In the spectrum shown, there is indeed a window of this size not usable.</p>\r\n<p>B. The first two pulses add up to zero, the second two pulses to 180°. The trick of this composite pulse lies in the compensation of B1 inhomogeneity as described in ref. [4], p. 265.</p>\r\n<p>C. There are no additional zero excitation points of this sequence, thus the NH region or the aliphatic region of the NMR spectra is unaffected.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-292":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>3.4 Solvent Suppression using WET</h4>\r\n<p>A. Because of the solvent gradient, the solvent chemical shifts and their differences vary. The software must compensate for this to maintain equally good suppression throughout the run, e.g. by a scout scan, automatic frequency-range detection, and subsequent pulse-shape calculation.</p>\r\n<p>B. See reference [4].</p>\r\n<p>C. These signals would also be suppressed, because the selective pulses saturate the water resonance and this saturation transfers to exchangeable protons.</p>\r\n<h4>3.5 SELTOCSY</h4>\r\n<p>A. During the spin lock, spins precess in the rotating frame only in the radio-frequency field, a very weak magnetic field. They therefore have almost no chemical-shift differences and all spin systems are of “higher order.” Every spin couples to every other spin provided there is an uninterrupted coupling chain. Spin couplings are independent of magnetic field.</p>\r\n<p>B. There are seven chemical bonds from the anomeric proton H-1′ to H-6′, but only five from H-3 to H-6.</p>\r\n<p>C. There is no significant spin coupling between H-1′ and any fructose proton.</p>\r\n<h4>3.6 SELNOESY</h4>\r\n<p>A. This can result from a three-spin effect in which one spin acts as a very effective relaxation sink for a nearby spin.</p>\r\n<p>B. Yes, without difficulty and—if only one type of proton is of interest—in much less time than 2D NOESY.</p>\r\n<p>C. H-13 is transoid with respect to H-8.</p>\r\n<h4>3.7 SELINCOR</h4>\r\n<p>A. After pulse p8 only <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>−</sup></span> is present; p9 converts it into <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>+</sup></span>. The negative value of gradient g3 multiplied by <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>−</sup></span> gives a positive contribution, which adds to <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>+</sup> × g4</span>. At the end of the sequence only <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup></span> is present. Since hydrogen has a four-times-higher gyromagnetic ratio than carbon, <span class=\"inline-equation\">(g3 × <i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>−</sup>) + (g4 × <i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>+</sup>) + (g5 × <i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup>) = 0</span>.</p>\r\n<p>B. GARP decoupling of carbons requires a power level that heats the probe head and must therefore be minimized.</p>\r\n<p>C. The sensitivity of this sequence is of course one hundred times lower than that of normal proton spectra. However, in crowded proton regions it is often impossible to irradiate individual protons, whereas carbon chemical-shift dispersion is usually larger.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-293":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>3.8 SELINQUATE</h4>\r\n<p>A. No, compared to the phase cycling employed here, a gradient DQF filter is less sensitive by a factor of 2, though this improves the suppression of unwanted signals.</p>\r\n<p>B. The aromatic CC spin coupling is about 60 Hz.</p>\r\n<p>C. Unlikely, since the three-spin couplings range from 35 to 60 Hz.</p>\r\n<h4>3.9 Band Selective HMBC</h4>\r\n<p>A. The 180° pulse should invert, e.g., from –y to +y, but not excite from z t to x, y plane.</p>\r\n<p>B. In oligopeptides the carboxyl groups usually resonate very closely together and are difficult to assign.</p>\r\n<p>C. No, carboxyl groups have no hydrogen attached, and therefore a breakthrough of 1JCH is not possible.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-294":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>Answers Chapter 4</h3>\r\n<h4>4.1 <sup>11</sup>B NMR Spectroscopy</h4>\r\n<p>A. This must be connected to the symmetry around the boron nuclei, which is apparently higher than in usual organic boron compounds.</p>\r\n<p>B. <sup>13</sup>C NMR signals of such carbon atoms should in principle give a 1:1:1:1 pattern of four multiplet lines, which, however, often coalesce due to the rapid relaxation of boron, and therefore only reveal a broad hump. If one lowers the temperature significantly one could reach a regime where the boron relaxation is slowed down. If one has an appropriate probe head, one can also try to perform boron single-frequency decoupling in addition to proton decoupling.</p>\r\n<p>C. The <sup>11</sup>B–<sup>19</sup>F spin coupling is smaller than 1 Hz.</p>\r\n<h4>4.2 <sup>15</sup>N NMR Spectroscopy</h4>\r\n<p>A. Very broad lines due to quadrupolar relaxation.</p>\r\n<p>B. As in the figure given with DEPT; however, the central transitions are missing.</p>\r\n<p>C. Yes. If one uses 10 Hz for the d2 delay, one often also obtains correlations for <sup>1</sup><i>J</i> spin couplings of 90 Hz.</p>\r\n<h4>4.3 <sup>17</sup>O NMR Spectroscopy</h4>\r\n<p>A. D<sub>2</sub>O is prepared by distillation of H<sub>2</sub>O, with the heavier isotopes enriched by the distillation process. Depending on the quality of the separation, D<sub>2</sub>O can therefore have a higher <sup>17</sup>O content.</p>\r\n<p>B. In view of the linewidth of <sup>17</sup>O signals, a decimal point does not make sense.</p>\r\n<p>C. The carbonyl oxygen is sp<sup>2</sup> hybridized and therefore, as in carbon or proton NMR, its signal is deshielded.</p>\r\n<h4>4.4 <sup>19</sup>F NMR Spectroscopy</h4>\r\n<p>A. The frequencies of <sup>1</sup>H and <sup>19</sup>F are so close that interferences have to be cut off.</p>\r\n<p>B. According to chemical-shift theory, the paramagnetic term of the chemical shielding is the dominant one for fluorine, in contrast to protons. In addition, the seven outer electrons are much more polarizable than the single electron in hydrogen.</p>\r\n<p>C. The reason is the very large chemical-shift range of <sup>19</sup>F; small isotope effects are therefore observed much better than with protons.</p>\r\n<h4>4.5 <sup>29</sup>Si NMR Spectroscopy</h4>\r\n<p>A. In principle 13 lines with the intensities 1 : 13 : 78 : 286 : 715 : 1145 : 1145 : 715 : 286 : 78 : 13 : 1.</p>\r\n<p>B. 4% of the main signal.</p>\r\n<p>C. Yes; see S. Berger, “INEPT-HMQC – A new pulse sequence for <sup>13</sup>C,<sup>29</sup>Si correlation,” <i>J. Magn. Reson. Ser. A</i> 1993, 101, 329–332.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-295":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>D. A multitude of signals, but less complicated than the corresponding <sup>13</sup>C NMR spectrum.</p>\r\n<h4>4.6 <sup>57</sup>Fe NMR Spectroscopy</h4>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-295-vector-auto-1\"><img alt=\"Pulse sequence and coherence-transfer pathway for the iron-57 NMR experiment.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/diagrams/pdf-295-vector-auto-1.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>A. On the 600 MHz instrument used, the carbon frequency SFO1 was 150.913 MHz and the iron frequency SFO3 was 19.461 MHz. At the first negative gradient g1 the coherence level is <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>+</sup><i>I</i><sub>Fe</sub><sup>+</sup> = 170.374</span>; for g2, <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>+</sup><i>I</i><sub>Fe</sub><sup>−</sup> = 131.452</span>; g3 gives <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>−</sup><i>I</i><sub>Fe</sub><sup>−</sup> = −170.374</span>; and g4 gives <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>−</sup><i>I</i><sub>Fe</sub><sup>+</sup> = −131.452</span>. If 80% is chosen for g1–g4, the magnetization is dephased by −80 × 170.374 + 80 × 131.452 + 80 × (−170.374) − 80 × (−131.452) = 6227.52 units. Since <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>−</sup></span> enters the receiver, it must be rephased by g5 using 6227.52/150.913 = 41.3%.</p>\r\n<p>B. In ferrocene the carbon atoms are coupled to their respective protons and the NOE is helpful. The reported Fe,C spin-coupling constant of 4.7 Hz would disappear within the 2D linewidth. Iron pentacarbonyl has no protons, but has a useful Fe,C spin coupling of 24 Hz that can be seen only without iron decoupling.</p>\r\n<h4>4.7 <sup>195</sup>Pt NMR Spectroscopy</h4>\r\n<p>A. The linewidth is too large, and hence the transverse relaxation time is too short to permit the delay 1/[2<i>J</i>(Pt,H)] required in an HMQC experiment.</p>\r\n<p>B. Spin-coupling constants of two isotopes to the same nucleus are in the ratio of their gyromagnetic ratios. γ(<sup>14</sup>N)/γ(<sup>15</sup>N) = 0.713; therefore <sup>1</sup><i>J</i>(<sup>195</sup>Pt,<sup>14</sup>N) should be 199.6 Hz.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-296":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>Answers Chapter 5</h3>\r\n<h4>5.1 Measurement of the Spin–Lattice Relaxation Time T<sub>1</sub></h4><p>A. Typically 2 to 3 s.</p>\r\n<p>B. Typically 15 s</p>\r\n<p>C. Typically 70 ms</p>\r\n<p>D. There are two usual problems:</p>\r\n<p>1) too short waiting time d1 and too short waiting times W. 2) The inversion property of the 180° pulse is offset-dependent.</p>\r\n<h4>5.2 Measurement of the Spin–Spin Relaxation Time T<sub>2</sub></h4><p>A. Using the equation in the main text gives Δν<sub>1/2</sub> = 0.03 Hz.</p>\r\n<p>B. It should not change.</p>\r\n<p>C. Quadrupolar nuclei relax very fast and the equation T1 = T2 holds. Their relaxation times are in the order of a few milliseconds.</p>\r\n<p>D. Dynamic exchange of nuclei due to chemical processes.</p>\r\n<h4>5.3 Dynamic 1H NMR Spectroscopy</h4>\r\n<p>A. There are no unconfirmed assumptions within the Arrhenius scheme. However, the activation energy is assumed to be temperature- independent and the frequency factor is not of much chemical use. Using the Eyring equation, ΔG<sup>‡</sup> can be reported for the standard temperature 298 K.</p>\r\n<p>B. The sample works via the temperature dependence of the hydrogen bonds. It must be electronically calibrated versus an external device.</p>\r\n<p>C. The typical frequency difference in NMR for exchanging spins is 10 to 1000 Hz, and from this a dynamic range of 20 to 100 kJ/ mol can be calculated for which line shape alterations can be observed. In IR spectroscopy one may see exchange between bands from 10 cm−1 to 100 cm−1, which corresponds to Δν of 10<sup>5</sup> to 10<sup>4</sup> Hz. Therefore the time scale of IR is two orders of magnitude faster.</p>\r\n<p>D. Chlorine is inductively electron withdrawing, but mesomerically contributing electrons. Due to the latter effect a C=N double bond contribution is less likely, which leads to a lower barrier.</p>\r\n<h4>5.4 Diffusion Measurement with DOSY</h4>\r\n<p>A. In the diffusion equation, the gyromagnetric ratio J occurs as a quadratic (i.e. squared) term in the exponent and thus is most important. The standard high resolution NMR spectrometers are not built for diffusion measurements and have only weak gradient pulses. To achieve a good dephasing, one therefore needs the nuclei with the highest gyromagnetic ratio.</p>\r\n<p>B. To avoid any gradient echo during the sequence with increasing gradient strength of g1, g2, g4 and g5.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-297":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>C. Yes, tetramethylsilane. See E. J. Cabrita, S. Berger, “DOSY studies of hydrogen bond association: TMS as a reference compound for diffusion studies,” <i>Magn. Reson. Chem.</i> 2001, 39, 142–148.</p>\r\n<p>D. In cases of anisotropic diffusion, e.g. rigid molecules with a long carbon chain or stretched gels.</p>\r\n<h4>5.5 Residual Dipolar Couplings (RDC)</h4>\r\n<p>A. Dipolar coupling constants are much larger than isotropic <i>J</i>-coupling values. If the analyte is aligned only very slightly, the dipolar coupling is nearly averaged to zero. In the solid state it is of the order of several kHz (e.g. about 4.5 kHz for a C–H pair in a methylene group); partial alignment reduces it to a residual value of a few hertz.</p>\r\n<p>B. In the solid state, quadrupolar nuclei display a Pake doublet with a large dipolar splitting. Slight anisotropic alignment therefore also induces a splitting, making it easy to determine whether orientation has occurred in the NMR tube.</p>\r\n<p>C. After the INEPT transfer, the <i>t</i><sub>1</sub> evolution of a C,H doublet starts from <span class=\"inline-equation\">−2<i>I</i><sub>Hy</sub><i>I</i><sub>Cz</sub></span>, producing an anti-phase doublet in F<sub>1</sub>. A methylene carbon initially forms two anti-phase doublets; the central line of the triplet consists of the right-hand line of the left doublet and the left-hand line of the right doublet. These cancel because they have opposite phase. The 180° pulse in an HSQC sequence creates in-phase doublets that would appear as a triplet, but because the pulse also reverses the oscillation direction, a singlet is ultimately observed.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-298":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>Answers Chapter 6</h3>\r\n<h4>6.1 ASIS</h4>\r\n<p>A. The signal of the OH proton in a solution of 10 mg cresol in 0.7 mL CDCl3 appears at G = 4.76; it is shielded in benzene by 0.82 ppm to G =3.94, but deshielded by 0.3 ppm to G = 5.06 in pyridine.</p>\r\n<p>Interestingly, the center of the aromatic AA'XX' system at G = 6.88 is also shielded in benzene by 0.2 ppm to G = 6.67, whereas this spin system collapses to a singlet at G = 7.15 in pyridine. The signal of the methyl group is shielded in benzene by 0.27 ppm to G = 2.07, whereas in pyridine only a small shielding by 0.04 ppm is observed.</p>\r\n<p>In contrast to the results of cresol, the acidic OH proton of salicylaldhyde is deshielded in benzene by 0.44 ppm to G = 11.47 and further by another 0.7 ppm in the basic solvent pyridine. The signal of the aldehyde proton is first shielded by 0.75 ppm in benzene, but deshielded by 0.52 ppm in pyridine.</p>\r\n<p>The center of the four aromatic protons is shielded in benzene by 0.6 ppm, but deshielded in pyridine by 0.1 ppm. The ABCD sequence of the chemical shifts in salicylaldehyde obtained in CDCl3 is changed by the aromatic solvents. Apparently, in pyridine two effects are operative, the aromatic solvent and its basic properties.</p>\r\n<p>B. See P. Lazlo and D.H. Williams, \"A variable-temperature nuclear magnetic resonance study of toluene-ketone complex\" J. Amer. Chem. Soc 1966, 88, 2799−2802.</p>\r\n<h4>6.2 Chirality Determination</h4>\r\n<p>A. 0.82 /about 20:1.</p>\r\n<p>B. 0.75 Hz and therefore still observable.</p>\r\n<h4>6.3 Advanced Mosher Methods</h4>\r\n<p>A. No, these two compounds are a pair of enantiomers and therefore give identical NMR spectra.</p>\r\n<p>B. The chemical shift differences are caused by the anisotropy of the phenyl ring and are therefore dependent on its distance.</p>\r\n<p>C. In principle yes, but secondary amides may display two different rotamers around the amide bond rendering the analysis somewhat more difficult.</p>\r\n<h4>6.4 Quantitative NMR and Relaxation Reagent</h4>\r\n<p>A. The methylene group of ethanol resonates in the region of sugar and aromatic signals and therefore might give a wrong integration.</p>\r\n<p>B. 0.1 mL 99.9 % D2O can contain 0.1 % HDO/H2O, and this corresponds to 0.0001 mL. In 0.7 mL of this Grappa are 0.44 mL H2O; thus the possible error will be 0.023 %.</p>\r\n<p>C. Due to paramagnetic relaxation Cr(acac)3 introduces line broadening, which affects the signal intensity.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-299":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>D. The overlapping signals stem from C-2 and C-3. If one draws circles around these carbon atoms, a rather slight environmental difference can only be seen in the third sphere. Line broadening by Cr(acac)3 causes these signals to overlap.</p>\r\n<h4>6.5 Determination of Association Constants <i>K</i><sub>a</sub></h4><p>A. UV and fluorescence spectroscopy.</p>\r\n<p>B. Diffusion constants are less reliable than frequency-based chemical shifts.</p>\r\n<h4>6.6 STD NMR</h4>\r\n<p>A. Measure an identical sample under identical conditions, but without any protein.</p>\r\n<p>B. Many proteins have absorption at frequencies lower than the TMS frequency. Otherwise one can irradiate the aromatic residues of a protein if the ligand has no aromatic signals.</p>\r\n<p>C. One obtains a build-up curve (similar to NOE build-up curves) by lengthening the irradiation time.</p>\r\n<h4>6.7 A Kinetic Experiment</h4>\r\n<p>A. The Michaelis–Menten equation is <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>v</i> = <i>v</i><sub>max</sub>[S]/(<i>K</i><sub>M</sub> + [S])</span>. Boundary conditions: steady state, [substrate] ≫ [enzyme], and an irreversible reaction from the enzyme–substrate complex to the enzyme–product complex.</p>\r\n<p>B. All signals must have been relaxed before they are excited in the next run, otherwise the kinetics would be in error.</p>\r\n<p>C. A 2D file ensures that all spectra are run identically. Also the processing, e.g., the phase correction, will be identical.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-300":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>Answers Chapter 7</h3><h4>7.1 The CP/MAS Experiment</h4>\r\n<p>A. One observes the FID of <sup>79</sup>Br under rapid pulsing. This may work on the <sup>13</sup>C channel without detuning because both NMR frequencies are nearby (e.g. at 600 MHz, the <sup>79</sup>Br frequency is only 540 kHz below <sup>13</sup>C). The FID is modulated by sharp sidebands. Adjust the magic-angle screw until these sidebands persist over the entire FID.</p>\r\n<p>B. To set the Hartmann–Hahn condition, the r.f. fields on protons and nucleus X (e.g. <sup>13</sup>C) must be equal, so their 90° pulse widths must be equal. Detect the proton signal of adamantane spinning at 4 kHz and set the power so the proton 90° pulse is 5–6 μs. Then observe the adamantane <sup>13</sup>C signal with decoupling at this power and set the <sup>13</sup>C power similarly. These values are starting points for Hartmann–Hahn adjustment. Keep the proton power constant and vary the <sup>13</sup>C power until the signal is maximal.</p>\r\n<p>C. The CO group has a much larger chemical-shift anisotropy.</p>\r\n<h4>7.2 High-Resolution Magic-Angle Spinning</h4>\r\n<p>A. The centrifugal acceleration of a mass point in circular motion with constant angular velocity is <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>a</i> = ω<sup>2</sup> × <i>r</i></span>. With <i>r</i> = 2 × 10<sup>−3</sup> m, ω = 2π<i>f</i>, and <i>f</i> = 8 kHz, one obtains ω<sup>2</sup> = 2.53 × 10<sup>9</sup> s<sup>−2</sup> and <i>a</i> = 5.05 × 10<sup>6</sup> m s<sup>−2</sup>.</p>\r\n<p>Comparison with gravitational acceleration on Earth (<i>g</i> = 9.81 m s<sup>−2</sup>) gives a factor of about half a million. Including a rotor wall thickness of 0.5 mm reduces the ratio to about 390000.</p>\r\n<p>B. Friction between the rotor outer wall and air leads to heat dissipation.</p>\r\n<p>C. Spinning at the magic angle modulates the dipolar interaction between two nuclei, which may change spin-relaxation behavior depending on spinning speed. The sample temperature may also change (cf. B). Finally, the enormous g forces (cf. A) may cause conformational changes.</p>\r\n<h4>7.3 HN HSQC</h4>\r\n<p>A. After the evolution period, both <span class=\"inline-equation\">2<i>I</i><sub>Hz</sub><i>I</i><sub>Nx</sub></span> and <span class=\"inline-equation\">2<i>I</i><sub>Hz</sub><i>I</i><sub>Ny</sub></span> are present. Pulses p15 and p6 with phase x convert only the latter into <span class=\"inline-equation\">2<i>I</i><sub>Hy</sub><i>I</i><sub>Nz</sub></span>. During [d3, p16, p7, d3] this develops into <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>Hx</sub></span>, which p8 stores as <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>Hz</sub></span>. At the same time p15 and p6 convert <span class=\"inline-equation\">2<i>I</i><sub>Hz</sub><i>I</i><sub>Nx</sub></span> into double-quantum magnetization <span class=\"inline-equation\">2<i>I</i><sub>Hy</sub><i>I</i><sub>Nx</sub></span>; p18 and p9 with phase y reconvert it into <span class=\"inline-equation\">2<i>I</i><sub>Hy</sub><i>I</i><sub>Nz</sub></span>. During [d3, p18, p9, d3] this develops into <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>Hx</sub></span>.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-301":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>The final 90° pulse p10 leaves this unchanged but converts the stored magnetization <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>Hz</sub></span> into <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>Hy</sub></span>; both original components are therefore retained.</p>\r\n<p>B. γ<sub>H</sub> and γ<sub>N</sub> are in a ratio of about 10:1. At gradient g2 the coherence pathway contains only <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>N</sub><sup>+</sup></span> or <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>N</sub><sup>−</sup></span>; at g5 only <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>N</sub><sup>−</sup></span> is present. For (g2 × <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>N</sub><sup>+</sup></span>) + (g5 × <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>N</sub><sup>−</sup></span>) = 0, the gradient ratio must therefore also be 10:1.</p>\r\n<p>C. Hardly. Two- and three-bond spin couplings of NH protons to <sup>13</sup>C are of the order of the proton linewidth. Pulse sequences that perform this task do exist, however.</p>\r\n<h4>7.4 HNCA</h4>\r\n<p>A. In a traditional evolution period, <i>t</i><sub>1</sub> or <i>t</i><sub>2</sub> increases throughout the period. Because relaxation is time-dependent, signals from the beginning have a different weight from those at the end; the same applies to spin-coupling contributions. In a constant-time scheme the 180° pulses move through the period, so all signals have the same weight, but all suffer the same relaxation losses. Constant-time periods are more difficult to program and set up.</p>\r\n<p>B. The effect is known from homonuclear decoupling. Irradiation of one proton shifts nearby proton resonances to higher frequencies. The additional decoupler field produces an effective B<sub>0</sub> field in the rotating frame that is larger than without decoupling.</p>\r\n<p>C.</p>\r\n<div class=\"appendix-table-wrap\"><table class=\"appendix-table\"><thead><tr><th>Chemical shifts</th><th>Assignments</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8.66 / 62.4 and 56.3</td><td><code>T12 and K11</code></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td>8.52 / 57.9 and 53.6</td><td><code>K63 and Q62</code></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td>8.19 / 56.6 and 55.9</td><td><code>E51 and L50</code></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td>7.87 / 59.8 and 55.4</td><td><code>K29 and A28</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-302":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>Answers Chapter 8</h3><h4>8.1 Calibration of Pulse Duration in the Transmitter Channel</h4>\r\n<p>A. These probes have the highest proton sensitivity. To obtain this they are designed with a high quality factor of the resonant circuits, giving a high induced voltage for response. The drawback is radiation damping if very strong signals are present.</p>\r\n<p>B. Since a pulse near 360° creates little transverse magnetization but returns magnetization to +z, long waiting times due to T<sub>1</sub> relaxation are unnecessary between experiments. Moreover, in Fig. 8.1-3 the nulls of the 360° pulse are more reliable than those of the 180° pulse.</p>\r\n<p>C. Biological buffers contain many inorganic salts, which strongly affect susceptibility and may induce eddy currents in the sample.</p>\r\n<h4>8.2 Calibration of the Pulse Duration in the Indirect Channel</h4>\r\n<p>A. The spectrum is inherently of higher order and must be considered as an A<sub>2</sub>, A<sub>2</sub>′, X, X′ spin system; see P. Stilbs, S. Forsén, “Spin-spin couplings in doubly <sup>15</sup>N labeled urea and 1,3-dimethylurea,” <i>Org. Magn. Reson.</i> 1976, 8, 384–386.</p>\r\n<p>B. Same result as with no decoupler pulse: an unchanged anti-phase doublet.</p><p>C.</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-302-indirect-channel-sequence\"><img alt=\"Pulse sequence and coherence-transfer pathway for indirect-channel pulse calibration.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/pulse-sequences/pdf-302-indirect-channel-sequence.webp\"/></figure>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-303":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>If p2 = 0, only the dashed lines apply. At the first gradient g1, <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>+</sup></span> is present, with coherence level −4 + 1 = −3. <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup></span> enters the receiver with coherence level −4. Thus g1 and g2 must be in the ratio 4:(−3) to satisfy (g1 × level 1) + (g2 × level 2) = 0.</p>\r\n<p>If p2 = 90°, the solid lines apply and no single-quantum coherence enters the receiver.</p>\r\n<h4>8.3 Shaped Pulses</h4>\r\n<p>A. “Transition”-selective: 1–2 Hz, SPT experiments. “Multiplet”-selective: 50 Hz, selective NOE or COSY experiments. “Band”-selective: 30 ppm, 3D protein NMR, selective pulses on all C=O or all C<sub>α</sub> carbon atoms.</p>\r\n<p>B. 1/[4 × 10 × 10<sup>−6</sup> s] = 25 kHz, i.e. suitable on a 400 MHz spectrometer; 1/[4 × 2 × 10<sup>−3</sup> s] = 125 Hz, i.e. sufficient for complete water suppression.</p>\r\n<p>C. If the <sup>13</sup>C signal is coupled to <sup>1</sup>H, it has a bandwidth of 3 × 130 Hz = 390 Hz; otherwise 10 Hz should be sufficient.</p>\r\n<h4>8.4 Adiabatic Pulses</h4>\r\n<p>A. From Wikipedia (August 2012, http://en.wikipedia.org): “Adiabatic processes can occur if the container of the system has thermally insulated walls or the process happens in an extremely short time, so that there is no opportunity for significant heat exchange. The term ‘adiabatic’ literally means impassable, coming from the Greek roots α- (‘not’), δια- (‘through’), and βαίνειν (‘to pass’); this etymology corresponds here to an absence of heat transfer.”</p>\r\n<p>B. Composite pulses; these are still used for decoupling and spin locking.</p>\r\n<h4>8.5 Temperature Calibration in NMR</h4>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-303-equation-2\"><img alt=\"Temperature-calibration equations for 340 K and 345 K.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-303-equation-2.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>The error is of the order of 1 kJ/mol.</p>\r\n<p>B. The required thermal insulation between the sample tube and the deeply cooled r.f. coil limits the temperature range. Cryoprobes have therefore been developed mainly for biological samples, since water freezes at 273 K.</p>\r\n<p>C. The Seyferth mixture of THF and dimethyl ether, both in deuterated form, is most popular in organic chemistry.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-304":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>8.6 Calibration of Pulsed Field Gradients</h4><p>A.</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-304-gradient-calibration-sequence\"><img alt=\"Pulse sequence for calibration of pulsed field gradients.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/pulse-sequences/pdf-304-gradient-calibration-sequence.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>B. Yes, and it gives the same result and works even when the transmitter offset is not on resonance, because chemical shift is refocused by the 180° pulse.</p>\r\n<p>C. Evolution periods are usually much longer than the gradient pulses, so short ring-down times are not mandatory.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n"},"chinese":{"pdf-283":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>第 1 章答案</h3><h4>1.1 <sup>1</sup>H NMR</h4>\r\n<p>A. H-4 的弛豫时间最长。 B. ABCD。 C. 屋脊效应。</p>\r\n<p>D. 这由 Karplus 方程决定；当二面角接近 90° 时，该方程预言邻位自旋耦合很小。</p>\r\n<h4>1.2 APT-<sup>13</sup>C-NMR</h4>\r\n<p>A. R. R. Ernst 计算了 FT NMR 实验中最佳脉冲角随重复时间和弛豫时间的变化。在给定边界条件下，Ernst 角可在一定时间内获得最佳信噪比。参见 R. R. Ernst、W. A. Anderson：“Application of 傅里叶 Transform Spectroscopy to Magnetic Resonance”， Rev. Sci. Instr. 1966, 37, 93–102。</p>\r\n<p>B. 这是 1 J CH 自旋耦合常数，通常取折中值 145 Hz。</p>\r\n<p>C. 复合脉冲去耦是按一定节律施加的一系列不同宽度、不同相位的射频脉冲。WALTZ-16 即其中一种方案；参见 A. J. Shaka、J. Keeler、T. Frenkiel、R. Freeman：“An improved sequence for broadband decoupling: WALTZ-16”， J. Magn. Reson. 1983, 52, 335–338。</p>\r\n<p>D. 氘的自旋量子数 I = 1，因此碳信号按氘自旋的 I z = −1、0、+1 分裂成三条近似 1:1:1 的谱线。可利用 J D 与 J H 的比值计算 J CH 。</p>\r\n<p>E. 羰基碳位于 δ C = 169.3 ppm，C-12 位于 δ C = 77.6 ppm。</p>\r\n<h4>1.3 COSY</h4>\r\n<p>A. 400 MHz 仪器上的典型 COSY 需覆盖 10 ppm 的质子化学位移范围，即 4 kHz。相应的 t 1 增量为 1/(4000 Hz) = 250 μs；256 个增量对应最大 t 1 为 64 ms。由 2 I 1x I 2z 到 2 I 1z I 2y 的 COSY 转移在 sin π Jt 1 = 1（即 Jt 1 = 0.5）时最佳。当 J = 7 Hz 时，7 Hz × 0.064 s ≈ 0.45，转移效率较高；若耦合仅约 1 Hz，则 1 Hz × 0.064 s ≈ 0.06，转移效率很低。可在混合脉冲前后加入固定延迟，使转移从较大的有效 t 1 开始。</p>\r\n<p>B. 间接维 F 1 的数字分辨率总是有限，因此自旋耦合常数应只从直接维 F 2 提取。</p>\r\n<p>C. 产生双量子磁化时，需要把已演化的反相磁化 2 I 1x I 2z 转换为 2 I 1x I 2y 。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-284":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>该过程只在较大的 <i>t</i><sub>1</sub> 值时有效，在演化初始阶段并不有效（参见 A）。</p>\r\n<p>D. 双量子滤波器会抑制单峰，同时把更复杂的自旋体系约化为 AX 情形，因此 DQF COSY 中所有交叉峰都呈 AX 型。</p>\r\n<p>E. 邻位自旋耦合由 Karplus 方程决定。C-15 的一个质子相对于 C16–H16 矢量的二面角接近 90°。</p>\r\n<h4>1.4 NOESY</h4>\r\n<p>A. 脉冲 p3 把自旋置于旋转坐标系的 −z 方向，随后它们以 T 1 为时间常数向平衡磁化弛豫。视化学位移而定，一些自旋已经进入正值区，只需一至两个 T 1 即可达到平衡。因此，约一个 T 1 的混合时间通常最适合产生有效交叉弛豫。</p>\r\n<p>B. 改变混合时间记录多张 NOESY 谱，并把交叉峰体积积分对混合时间作图，即得到增长曲线。对最初几个点作线性拟合称为初始速率近似。</p>\r\n<p>C. 只有在增长曲线最初的线性区，才能假设与距离相关的两自旋成对相互作用。混合时间变长后，其他自旋开始参与，自旋扩散也可能降低积分值；在很长混合时间下，多数自旋已接近平衡，不再给出交叉弛豫信息。</p>\r\n<p>D. NOESY 交叉弛豫从 −z（布居反转）开始，直到自旋达到 +z 平衡磁化；ROESY 从零（等布居）开始，因此动态范围只有前者的一半。</p>\r\n<p>E. 可选用非对映异位亚甲基质子或邻位芳香质子，其间距可由几何关系方便计算；若附近还有其他质子则需谨慎。</p>\r\n<h4>1.5 HSQC</h4>\r\n<p>A. HMQC 只需四个射频脉冲，而 HSQC 需要十个，因此对调节误差更敏感。不过在 HMQC 的 t 1 期间，由于质子自旋也处于横向状态，H,H 耦合同样发生演化，导致 F 1 线宽增加，并降低拥挤区的分辨率。</p>\r\n<p>B. 乘积算符分析表明，对于 CH 2 基团，双重反向转移会抵消 CH 基团中可观察到的灵敏度增益。</p>\r\n<p>亚甲基可用两个质子自旋 <i>I</i><sub>H1</sub>、<i>I</i><sub>H2</sub> 和一个碳自旋 <i>I</i><sub>C</sub> 描述。以下计算忽略含 cos π<i>J</i><sub>CH</sub>d5 = 0 的项，只保留含 sin π<i>J</i><sub>CH</sub>d5 = 1 的项。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-285":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>INEPT 转移后，相关项为 <span class=\"inline-equation\">−2<i>I</i><sub>H1z</sub><i>I</i><sub>Cy</sub></span> 和 <span class=\"inline-equation\">−2<i>I</i><sub>H2z</sub><i>I</i><sub>Cy</sub></span>。二者在 <i>t</i><sub>1</sub> 期间按下式发生碳化学位移演化：</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-1\"><img alt=\"HSQC 答案中的第 1 组乘积算符方程。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-1.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>这两个项都进入双重 INEPT 反向转移。前两个 INEPT 脉冲 p16 和 p7 将它们转换为</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-2\"><img alt=\"HSQC 答案中的第 2 组乘积算符方程。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-2.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>随后发生 <i>J</i><sub>CH</sub> 演化；由于这里是三自旋体系，会得到更复杂的项：</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-3\"><img alt=\"HSQC 答案中的第 3 组乘积算符方程。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-3.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>脉冲 p18 和 p9 将这些项转换为</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-4\"><img alt=\"HSQC 答案中的第 4 组乘积算符方程。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-4.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>第二个自旋回波期间后续的 <i>J</i><sub>CH</sub> 演化得到</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-5\"><img alt=\"HSQC 答案中的第 5 组乘积算符方程。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-5.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>所有含 sin Ω<sub>C</sub><i>t</i> 调制的可观测项还含有 cos π<i>J</i><sub>CH</sub>d5 = 0 的分量，因而相互抵消。最后的 p11 脉冲产生可观测磁化：</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-285-equations-6\"><img alt=\"HSQC 答案中的第 6 组乘积算符方程。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-285-equations-6.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>这与 CH 基团的结果不同。因此，在本例中无法获得灵敏度增强，也不能保留等效转移路径。常用的折中办法是把 d5 从 1/4<i>J</i><sub>CH</sub> 改为 1/8<i>J</i><sub>CH</sub>，以兼顾甲基和亚甲基信号。</p>\r\n<p>C. 最佳水峰抑制通常通过经由异核的转移路径实现，因此一般无需额外的水峰抑制方案。</p>\r\n<p>D. HSQC 是“异核单量子相干”（Heteronuclear Single Quantum Coherence）的缩写。在这里， t 1 期间存在诸如 −2 I Hy I Cz 的项；与 HMQC 不同，只有一种自旋（即 13 C）处于横向状态。</p>\r\n<p>E. 90° 脉冲的频率激发范围远优于时长为其两倍的 180° 脉冲。</p>\r\n<p>F. 原则上，只要分子含有手性中心，所有亚甲基都具有非对映异位性；其差异程度取决于它们与立体中心的距离。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-286":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>1.6 HMBC</h4>\r\n<p>A. p1 与 p5 之间的延迟远长于 HSQC 中相应的延迟，因此弛豫效应可能十分关键。由于未采用 13 C 去耦，原则上所有相关信号都是双峰，而不是尖锐单峰。从质子到碳的转移只对部分信号达到最佳条件。</p>\r\n<p>B. 125–170 Hz；未计入 sp 杂化碳原子。</p>\r\n<p>C. 由于邻位含氮取代基的影响，C-4 的自旋耦合常数特别大（168 Hz）。类似地，C-17 处于完全脂肪族环境中，因此耦合常数较小（133 Hz）。</p>\r\n<p>D. 最后一个碳脉冲 p7 产生反相磁化；在采集期间，它发展为同相磁化，但对应不同的自旋耦合常数。此外，F1 中还受到 H,H 自旋耦合的调制。所有这些因素都会导致相位畸变。</p>\r\n<p>E. 第一个碳脉冲 p3 位于按耦合常数范围低端优化的延迟之后；第二个碳脉冲 p4 位于按该范围高端优化的延迟之后。长程自旋耦合不会受到这两个脉冲的显著影响。</p>\r\n<p>梯度 g1–g3 的总作用为零。因此，在 g3 之后，只有自 g1 开始时起保持原相干级的信号分量会重新聚焦，即长程相干。<sup>1</sup><i>J</i> 相干会因 p3 和 p4 改变相干级，因而被 g2 和 g3 进一步去相。</p>\r\n<p>F. 在梯度 g4 之前，相干级为 I H − I N + ；p6 将其变为 I H − I N − 。若质子与氮的旋磁比之比取 10:1，则前者对应 −9，后者对应 −11。g4 取 80% 时，前一信号的去相量为 80 × (−9) = −720 单位。因此，为仅通过 1 H– 15 N 相关，g5 应为 −720/−11 = 65.45%。在回波/反回波模式下若要先选择 I H − I N − 、再选择 I H − I N + ，应先把 g4 设为 −65.45%，g5 设为 80%。</p>\r\n<p>G. 在现代高场谱仪上， 13 C 谱宽超过 20 kHz。普通硬 180° 脉冲不足以使谱宽两端的碳信号同时重新聚焦，因此需采用扫频脉冲（参见第 8.4 章“Chirped Pulses”）。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-287":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>第 2 章答案</h3>\r\n<h4>2.1 二维 <i>J</i> 分解 <sup>1</sup>H NMR 波谱</h4>\r\n<p>A. 由于与德自旋耦合合,CHD2CD3S=O中的质子显示出一个五,其强度为1: 2: 3: 2: 1 .对于旋转I = 1的德旋转,每一个德核都具有三个磁旋转数 mz= −1, 0, +1,它们结合在五个总体旋转数 −2, −1, 0, +1, +2. 在2DJ解析谱图中,F1中中线的信号保持不变,因为这是一个异核自旋耦合.</p>\r\n<p>B. 考虑两个质子,有不同的化学位移,并相连,在正常1DNMR 谱图中形成AX自旋体系.在p1激动后,化学位移和自旋耦合在t1期间演变.化学位移由180°脉冲p2重新聚焦.在t1末,我们在乘积算符形式体系方面考虑只有旋转1:</p>\r\n<p class=\"display-equation\"><span class=\"inline-equation\">−2<i>I</i><sub>1x</sub><i>I</i><sub>2z</sub> sin(2π<i>J</i><sub>12</sub><i>t</i><sub>1</sub>) + <i>I</i><sub>1y</sub> cos(2π<i>J</i><sub>12</sub><i>t</i><sub>1</sub>)</span></p>\r\n<p>在随后的采集中，所有信号会同时受到自旋耦合常数的正弦和余弦调制，即吸收型与色散型线形相互叠加，从而形成扭曲的线形。</p>\r\n<p>C. 这是一种常见的 EXORCYCLE 阶段方案,它可以缓解脉冲不完美,即与名义脉冲相比,脉冲过于短或过于长.</p>\r\n<p>D. 自旋回波序列在自旋回波点上消除了化学位移的贡献,并通过模拟方式消除了磁场的不均性,导致线宽展宽.因此,F1中的多重点显示了它们的自然线条宽度.</p>\r\n<h4>2.2 ROESY</h4>\r\n<p>A. 考虑一个最初沿 −y 方向的自旋。脉冲 p2 将其转到 +y 轴，而脉冲 p3 又将其转回 −y 轴。因此，自旋锁定的相位循环始终使自旋重新取向于 xy 平面，从而防止快速去相，并减小 180° 脉冲的误差。</p>\r\n<p>B. 在ROESY中,只应混合弱弛豫条件.Toxy混合需要更强的混合脉冲,因为必须考虑自旋耦合条件.</p>\r\n<p>C. -8 和 H-13 均以相反的方向进行,因此交叉弛豫率将非常低,但自旋耦合率相当大.</p>\r\n<p>D. -1的信号与H-17和H-16的交叉弛豫.只有H-1,但不是H-4在这些旋转的范围内.</p>\r\n<h4>2.3 TOCSY</h4>\r\n<p>A. Spin lock: I 0 = 100 W; 10 log( I 0 / I ) = 12.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-288":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>log (<i>I</i><sub>0</sub>/<i>I</i>) = 1.2; <i>I</i><sub>0</sub>/<i>I</i> = 15.84, <i>I</i> = 6.31 W; 6.31 W × 0.3 s = 1.893 W s.</p>\r\n<p>Hard pulse: 100 W × 10 × 10<sup>−6</sup> s = 10<sup>−3</sup> W s.</p>\r\n<p>持续 300 ms 的一次自旋锁脉冲所耗散的能量，约为单个硬脉冲的 2000 倍。</p>\r\n<p>B. TOCSY 转移会使交叉峰产生相位畸变，因此宜采用幅值模式处理。</p>\r\n<p>C. 不同自旋锁方案覆盖谱宽的有效性不同。文献报道 MLEV16 自旋锁的系数为 1.7；例如不能只按 4000 Hz 计算，而应采用 4000 × 1.7 = 6800 Hz。由 p 1 = 1/(4 × 6800 s −1 ) 可得单个 90° 脉冲宽度为 36.8 μs。</p>\r\n<p>D.</p><dl class=\"appendix-definitions\"><dt>MLEV</dt><dd>Malcom Levitt</dd><dt>DIPSI</dt><dd>标量相互作用存在时的去耦</dd><dt>GARP</dt><dd>全局交替矩形脉冲</dd></dl>\r\n<h4>2.4 HSQC-TOCSY</h4><p>A.</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-288-hsqc-tocsy-pathway\"><img alt=\"HSQC-TOCSY 答案中的乘积算符演化路径。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-288-hsqc-tocsy-pathway.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>B. 在每个微调脉冲 p3 和 p6 之前，质子磁化均沿 x 方向；因此，只要两个微调脉冲从相同方向作用，目标磁化就不受影响。</p>\r\n<p>C. 与问题 B 的论证相同。在自旋锁开始前，质子磁化沿 y 方向，因而会被自旋锁捕获。</p>\r\n<p>D. 这是众所周知的异头效应所致，即氧原子孤对电子的相互作用。根据 Karplus 方程，在该构型中只应出现较小的耦合常数，因为 H-1′ 与 H-2′ 彼此处于赤道位/轴向位关系。</p>\r\n<h4>2.5 HOESY</h4>\r\n<p>A. 6 Li 的弛豫时间很长，而质子的弛豫时间短得多。在混合时间 d3 内发生交叉弛豫时，质子很可能在与 6 Li 建立有效关联之前已经充分弛豫。</p>\r\n<p>B. 带有一个或多个氢的碳原子通常无需借助与远距离质子的交叉弛豫，因此该实验主要对季碳有价值；前者的结构问题通常更适合用 HMBC 或相关方法研究。</p>\r\n<p>C. 梯度比 g1:g3 必须等于这些核的 NMR 频率之比。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-289":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>2.6 INADEQUATE</h4>\r\n<p>A. 在天然丰度度中,同一分子中13C,13C对的发生率为1.1% ×1.1%,因此,INADEQUATE只能检测到给定的度的10−4.</p>\r\n<p>B. 由于我们正在测量13C,因此结碳原子之间的光谱距离可以达到200ppm,例如,在600MHz谱仪上,这相当于30KHz.正常的硬 180°脉冲不覆盖这种频率差异.</p>\r\n<p>C. 在 g1 作用时存在双量子相干，因此 g1 的作用加倍；在 g2 作用时存在单量子相干，因此为实现重聚，g2 的强度必须为 g1 的两倍。</p>\r\n<p>D.</p>\r\n<h4>2.7 ADEQUATE</h4>\r\n<p>A. INADEQUATE 以碳灵敏度检测浓度的 10 −4 ，ADEQUATE 则以质子灵敏度检测相同的量。根据 R. R. Ernst、G. Bodenhausen 和 A. Wokaun 的 Principles of NMR in One and Two Dimensions （Clarendon Press, Oxford, 1987），第 468 页，二维实验灵敏度正比于 γ exc γ obs 3/2 。INADEQUATE 中两个 γ 都对应碳，得到 1 × 1 3/2 = 1；ADEQUATE 中二者都对应质子，得到 4 × 4 3/2 = 32。</p>\r\n<p>B. 104,因为在天然丰度的13C-13C对的可能性是 (1.1*10−2)*(1.1*10−2) 所有其他信号必须被抑制.</p>\r\n<p>C. -23a 和 H-23b 结合于同一碳原子 GC = 64.6; C-22 在 GC = 127.34 产生共,从而产生191.94 的 ADEQUA- TE值. H-20a 和 H-20b 结合于 GC = 52.68; C-21 的 GC = 140.45 增加到 193.13.</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure source-figure-wide\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-289-fig-1\"><img alt=\"源 PDF 第 289 页图像。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/spectra/pdf-289-fig-1.webp\"/></figure>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside class=\"source-sidebar\">\r\n<div class=\"source-sidebar-label\">原书侧栏</div>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-289-vector-auto-1\"><img alt=\"源 PDF 第 289 页图像。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/chemical-structures/pdf-289-vector-auto-1.webp\"/></figure>\r\n</aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-290":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>2.8 J-HMBC</h4>\r\n<p>A. 虽然这是2JCH自旋耦合,但包括氧原子及其自由电子对的二角将变得重要,并影响这种自旋耦合.</p>\r\n<p>B. 根据分子模型 (见 http://csi.chemie.tu-darmstadt.de/ak/immel/structures-nmr.html) C-10 和 H-12 之间的二角是 −113°;因此,根据卡普勒方程,3JCH 应该非常小.</p>\r\n<p>C. 在\"常数时间\"脉冲序列中,在第一次激起和检测开始之间总是存在相同的延迟,而在\"正常\"脉冲序列中,这种延迟从短到长的值增加.由于在常数时间中弛豫,人们必须面临信号损失.</p>\r\n<p>D. 在这种情况下,通过质子化学位移在F1中进行额外调节,JCH双重对彼此有抵消.</p>\r\n<h4>2.9 门控去耦</h4>\r\n<p>A. 宽分双源于C-12,其长距离CH自旋耦合由H-13,H-11和H-23的,双源于残余CHCl3信号.</p>\r\n<p>B. C-17的质子具有相同的相对化学位移.C-17的信号因此分为三分,由C-18的质子引起的额外长距离三分.相反,C-11的质子显示出高度的异性,并导致与C-11明显不同的1JCH自旋耦合.因此,我们观察到双重的双重.</p>\r\n<p>C. C-3 固定在芳香环中,显示了1JCH到自己的质子H-3,3JCH到H-1和进一步的小分离,可能是H-4.C-22被五个类似的3JCH的质子包围;此外,分子的这个部分可能有一定的灵活性.</p>\r\n<p>D. 从HMBC谱中可以看到,C=O原子对合H-11a/b和H-12.我们使用以下质子参数找到最好的协议:GH-11a = 3.132,GH-11b = 2.670,GH-12 = 4.288,2JH-11a,H-11b = −17.34 Hz,3JH-11a,H-12 = 3.34 Hz和3JH-11b,H-12 = 8.47 Hz. 根据这些作为质子基和C=O原子的化学位移在169.2我们使用2JC,H-11a=8Hz,2JC,H-11b=6.3,和3JC,H-12=1.9Hz,以提供完美的一致.</p>\r\n<p>E. NOE 效应的建立与衰减遵循相同的时间常数，因此最好将这两个时间设为相等。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-291":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>答案 第3章</h3>\r\n<h4>3.1 通过过度和水峰抑制</h4>\r\n<p>A. 水,分子量为18个,本身为55.55个摩拉,由于两个质子,它将产生相对NMR 信号111.11个单位,由于混合物是90%H2O/10%D2O,相对信号100个单位.因此,在这种混合物中的2mmol糖溶液将对1个质子的相对整体为2*10−5,这比水的整体少5000倍.</p>\r\n<p>B. 由于度转移到NH质子.</p>\r\n<p>C. 由于cw辐射只能用于一个频率,所以在多次度的预饱和延迟期间,有一些技术可以循环通过一组频率.</p>\r\n<p>D. AA' BB' CC'</p>\r\n<h4>3.2 溶剂抑制使用1D-NOESY</h4>\r\n<p>A. 一维 NOESY 的第一次采集基本相当于一个 270° 脉冲，即 90°x、90°x、90°x，脉冲之间带有延迟。第二次采集为 90°−x、90°x、90°x；除第一个脉冲的 −x 相位外，与第一种方案相同。然而，对于完整经历预定脉冲作用的任何共振，其净结果只是一个 90°x 脉冲。接收器用第一次扫描减去第二次扫描。线圈中心以外的共振在两次扫描中的响应不同，最终形成体积限制：来自样品两端、处于不均匀 B0 场中的信号被抵消，见文献［4］。</p>\r\n<p>B. 长时间的T1溶剂将禁止它在p2后完全恢复.</p>\r\n<p>C. 在时间的两点,磁化应在z方向上进行.Z-梯度破坏了以前扫描 (即\"破坏梯度\") 剩下的虚假xy磁化.</p>\r\n<h4>3.3 水峰抑制,用SOGGY激发雕刻</h4>\r\n<p>A. 90°脉冲则长达1ms.这种脉冲的强度,以频率单位表达为JB1/2S = 1/(4p90) = 1/0.004 = 250Hz,这大致说是其兴奋带宽.在所示谱图中,确实有一个尺寸不使用的窗口.</p>\r\n<p>B. 两个第一脉冲的数值为零,第二两个脉冲为180°.这种复合脉冲的诡计在于补偿B1不均性,如上述 [4] 页 265.</p>\r\n<p>C. 本序列没有额外的零刺激点,因此NMR谱的NH区域或脂肪族区域不受影响.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-292":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>3.4 使用 WET 的溶剂峰抑制</h4>\r\n<p>A. 由于存在溶剂梯度，溶剂的化学位移及其差值会发生变化。软件必须补偿这一效应，才能在整个实验过程中维持同等良好的溶剂峰抑制；例如可采用侦察扫描、自动检测频率范围，并据此计算脉冲形状。</p>\r\n<p>B. 见参考文献 [4]。</p>\r\n<p>C. 这些信号也会被抑制，因为选择性脉冲会饱和水共振，而这种饱和会转移到可交换质子。</p>\r\n<h4>3.5 SELTOCSY</h4>\r\n<p>A. 在自旋锁期间，自旋在旋转坐标系中只围绕射频场进动，也就是处于很弱的磁场中。因此它们几乎没有化学位移差异，所有自旋体系都呈“高阶”特征。只要存在连续的耦合链，每个自旋都可与其他自旋耦合；自旋耦合本身与磁场强度无关。</p>\r\n<p>B. 从异头质子 H-1′ 到 H-6′ 有七个化学键，而从 H-3 到 H-6 只有五个。</p>\r\n<p>C. H-1′ 与任何果糖质子之间均无显著自旋耦合。</p>\r\n<h4>3.6 SELNOESY</h4>\r\n<p>A. 这可能源于三自旋效应，其中一个自旋充当邻近自旋的高效弛豫汇。</p>\r\n<p>B. 可以，而且没有困难；如果只关心某一类质子，所需时间比二维 NOESY 短得多。</p>\r\n<p>C. H-13 相对于 H-8 为反式取向。</p>\r\n<h4>3.7 SELINCOR</h4>\r\n<p>A. 脉冲 p8 后只存在 I C − ，p9 将其转换为 I C + 。负梯度 g3 与 I C − 相乘产生正贡献，并与 I C + × g4 相加。序列末端只有 I H − 。由于氢的旋磁比约为碳的四倍，应满足 (g3 × I C − ) + (g4 × I C + ) + (g5 × I H − ) = 0 。</p>\r\n<p>B. 对碳进行 GARP 去耦所需的功率会使探头升温，因此必须尽量降低。</p>\r\n<p>C. 该序列的灵敏度当然比普通质子谱低约一百倍。不过，在拥挤的质子区往往无法单独照射某个质子，而碳的化学位移分散通常更大。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-293":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>3.8 SELINQUATE</h4>\r\n<p>A. 不,相比于这里使用的相循环,梯度DQF过器的敏感度是2倍的,</p>\r\n<p>B. 芳香的CC自旋耦合约为60Hz.</p>\r\n<p>C. 不太可能,因为三旋接的范围从35 Hz到60 Hz.</p>\r\n<h4>3.9 带选择性HMBC</h4>\r\n<p>A. The 180° pulse should invert, e.g., from –y to +y, but not excite from z t to x, y plane.</p>\r\n<p>B. 在寡素中,碳基基组通常非常紧密共,难以分配.</p>\r\n<p>C. 否,碳基基组没有,因此无法突破1JCH.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-294":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>第 4 章答案</h3>\r\n<h4>4.1 <sup>11</sup>B NMR 波谱</h4>\r\n<p>A. 这应与硼核周围的对称性有关；该对称性显然高于普通有机硼化合物。</p>\r\n<p>B. 原则上，这类碳原子的 13 C NMR 信号应显示四条谱线、强度比为 1:1:1:1 的多重峰。但由于硼弛豫很快，这些谱线常会合并，只表现为宽峰。显著降低温度可能进入硼弛豫变慢的区域。若有合适的探头，除质子去耦外，还可尝试对硼进行单频去耦。</p>\r\n<p>C. 11 B– 19 F 自旋耦合小于 1 Hz。</p>\r\n<h4>4.2 <sup>15</sup>N NMR 波谱</h4>\r\n<p>A. 四极弛豫使谱线非常宽。</p>\r\n<p>B. 与 DEPT 图中所示情况类似，但缺少中心跃迁。</p>\r\n<p>C. 可以。若将 d2 延迟按 10 Hz 设定，常也能得到对应于 90 Hz 的 1 J 自旋耦合相关。</p>\r\n<h4>4.3 <sup>17</sup>O NMR 波谱</h4>\r\n<p>A. D 2 O 由 H 2 O 蒸馏制备，蒸馏过程会富集较重的同位素。因此，根据分离质量，D 2 O 中的 17 O 含量可能高于 H 2 O。</p>\r\n<p>B. 考虑到 17 O 信号的线宽，报告小数位没有意义。</p>\r\n<p>C. 缰基氧采用 sp 2 杂化，因此与碳或质子 NMR 中的情况一样，其信号处于去屏蔽位置。</p>\r\n<h4>4.4 <sup>19</sup>F NMR 波谱</h4>\r\n<p>A. 1 H 与 19 F 的频率非常接近，因此必须滤除相互干扰。</p>\r\n<p>B. 根据化学位移理论，与质子不同，氟的化学屏蔽以顺磁项为主。此外，氟的七个外层电子比氢的单个电子更易极化。</p>\r\n<p>C. 原因是 19 F 的化学位移范围非常宽，因此微小的同位素效应比在质子谱中更容易观察。</p>\r\n<h4>4.5 <sup>29</sup>Si NMR 波谱</h4>\r\n<p>A. 原则上得到 13 条谱线，强度比为 1 : 13 : 78 : 286 : 715 : 1145 : 1145 : 715 : 286 : 78 : 13 : 1。</p>\r\n<p>B. 主信号的 4%。</p>\r\n<p>C. 可以；参见 S. Berger，“INEPT-HMQC – A new pulse sequence for 13 C, 29 Si correlation,” J. Magn. Reson. Ser. A 1993, 101, 329–332。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-295":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>D. 信号很多，但比相应的 13 C NMR 谱简单。</p>\r\n<h4>4.6 <sup>57</sup>Fe NMR 波谱</h4>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-295-vector-auto-1\"><img alt=\"57Fe NMR 实验的脉冲序列与相干转移路径。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/diagrams/pdf-295-vector-auto-1.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>A. 所用 600 MHz 仪器的碳频率 SFO1 为 150.913 MHz，铁频率 SFO3 为 19.461 MHz。在第一个负梯度 g1 处，相干级为 I C + I Fe + = 170.374 ；g2 处为 I C + I Fe − = 131.452 ；g3 处为 I C − I Fe − = −170.374 ；g4 处为 I C − I Fe + = −131.452 。若 g1–g4 均取 80%，磁化的去相量为 −80 × 170.374 + 80 × 131.452 + 80 × (−170.374) − 80 × (−131.452) = 6227.52 单位。进入接收器的是 I C − ，因此应以 g5 = 6227.52/150.913 = 41.3% 使其重新聚焦。</p>\r\n<p>B. 在二茂铁中，碳原子与各自的质子耦合，NOE 有助于提高信号。已报道的 Fe,C 耦合常数为 4.7 Hz，在二维谱线宽中会被淹没。五羰基铁不含质子，但有 24 Hz 的 Fe,C 耦合；只有不进行铁去耦时才能观察到。</p>\r\n<h4>4.7 <sup>195</sup>Pt NMR 波谱</h4>\r\n<p>A. 谱线过宽，横向弛豫时间因而太短，无法容纳 HMQC 实验所需的 1/[2 J (Pt,H)] 延迟。</p>\r\n<p>B. 两种同位素与同一核的自旋耦合常数之比，等于它们旋磁比之比。γ( 14 N)/γ( 15 N) = 0.713，因此 1 J ( 195 Pt, 14 N) 应为 199.6 Hz。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-296":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>答案 第五章</h3>\r\n<h4>5.1 自旋—晶格弛豫时间 T<sub>1</sub> 的测量</h4><p>A. 通常为 2–3 s。</p>\r\n<p>B. 通常是15秒</p>\r\n<p>C. 通常70 ms</p>\r\n<p>D. 常见的问题有两个:</p>\r\n<p>1) 太短的等待时间 d1 和太短的等待时间 W. 2) 180°脉冲的逆转性因偏移而取决.</p>\r\n<h4>5.2 自旋—自弛豫时间 T<sub>2</sub> 的测量</h4><p>A. 由正文中的方程可得 Δν<sub>1/2</sub> = 0.03 Hz。</p>\r\n<p>B. 它不应该改变.</p>\r\n<p>C. 四极核非常快速弛豫,方程T1=T2保持.它们的弛豫时间在几毫秒的顺序中.</p>\r\n<p>D. 由于化学过程,核的动态交换.</p>\r\n<h4>5.3 动态 1H NMR 波谱</h4>\r\n<p>A. 亚里尼斯计划中没有未经证实的假设.然而,被假定激活能量是温度独立的,频率因子没有很大的化学用途.使用 Eyring 方程，可报告标准温度 298 K 下的 ΔG ‡ .</p>\r\n<p>B. 样品通过结的温度依赖进行处理. 必须通过电子方式对外部设备进行校准.</p>\r\n<p>C. 转换转换的NMR中典型频率差异为10至1000Hz,从此可计算20至100kJ/mol的动态范围,可观察到线形变化.在IR光谱中,可以看到10cm−1到100cm−1之间的频段之间的交换,这相当于 Δν 为 10 5 –10 4 Hz.因此,IR的时间尺度是两次更快.</p>\r\n<p>D. 是引入式引电子,但是增量性贡献电子.由于后者效应,C=N双键贡献的可能性较小,导致较低的屏障.</p>\r\n<h4>5.4 用 DOSY 测量扩散</h4>\r\n<p>A. 在扩散方程中,旋磁比J在指数中作为一个方形 (即方形) 术语出现,因此是最重要的.标准高分辨率NMR 波谱仪不用于扩散测量,只有弱的梯度脉冲.为了实现良好的脱节,因此需要具有最高的旋磁比的核.</p>\r\n<p>B. 为避免在序列中形成任何梯度回波，g1、g2、g4 和 g5 的梯度强度应依次增大。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-297":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>C. 是，四甲基硅烷。参见 E. J. Cabrita、S. Berger：“DOSY studies of hydrogen bond association: TMS as a reference compound for diffusion studies”， Magn. Reson. Chem. 2001, 39, 142–148。</p>\r\n<p>D. 适用于各向异性扩散，例如带长碳链的刚性分子或拉伸凝胶。</p>\r\n<h4>5.5 残余偶极耦合（RDC）</h4>\r\n<p>A. 偶极耦合常数远大于各向同性 J 耦合。若分析物分子只发生很弱的取向，偶极耦合几乎会被平均为零。固态中的偶极耦合量级为数千赫兹（例如亚甲基中一对 C–H 约为 4.5 kHz），部分取向后只剩数赫兹的残余值。</p>\r\n<p>B. 固态四极核会给出具有大偶极分裂的 Pake 双峰。因此，轻微的各向异性取向也会诱发信号分裂，据此可方便判断 NMR 样品管内是否发生了取向。</p>\r\n<p>C. INEPT 转移后，C,H 双峰的 t 1 演化从 −2 I Hy I Cz 开始，在 F 1 维形成反相双峰。亚甲基碳最初形成两个反相双峰；三重峰的中央线由左侧双峰的右线和右侧双峰的左线组成，二者相位相反而相互抵消。HSQC 序列中的 180° 脉冲会产生同相双峰，本应表现为三重峰；但该脉冲还会反转振荡方向，因此最终观察到单峰。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-298":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>答案 第六章</h3>\r\n<h4>6.1 ASIS</h4>\r\n<p>A. 在0.7毫升CDCl3中10毫克水晶溶液中的OH质子的信号出现在G=4.76处;在素中,它在0.82ppm到G=3.94处被屏蔽,但在皮里丁中,在G=5.06处被屏蔽0.3ppm.</p>\r\n<p>有趣的是,芳香AA'XX'系统的中心在G=6.88也在中保卫0.2ppm到G=6.67,而这种自旋体系在中崩到G=7.15的单体.甲基组的信号在中保卫0.27ppm到G=2.07的信号,而在中仅观察到0.04ppm的小保卫.</p>\r\n<p>与体结果不同,酸OH质子的OH质子在中被0.44ppm解,G=11.47次,在基本溶剂皮里丁中再被0.7ppm解. aldehyde质子的信号首先在中被0.75ppm解,但在皮里丁中被0.52ppm解.</p>\r\n<p>四个芳香质子的中心在中保存在0.6ppm,但在中被保存在0.1ppm.在CDCl3中获得的基中化学位移的ABCD序列由芳香溶剂改变.显然,在中有两个作用,是芳香溶剂及其基本特性.</p>\r\n<p>B. 见P. Lazlo和D.H. Williams, \"一个变化温度核磁共振研究的氨基子复合物\"J. Amer. Chem. Soc 1966, 88, 2799−2802.</p>\r\n<h4>6.2 奇拉性确定性</h4>\r\n<p>A. 0.82/大约20:1</p>\r\n<p>B. 0.75 Hz,因此仍然可观察.</p>\r\n<h4>6.3 莫舍的先进方法</h4>\r\n<p>A. 不,这两个化合物是两对恩抗体,因此它们具有相同的NMR 波谱.</p>\r\n<p>B. 化学位移是由基环的异性化引起的,因此它们取决于距离.</p>\r\n<p>C. 原则上是的,但二次胺可能会在胺结周围显示两个不同的旋转器,使分析变得更困难.</p>\r\n<h4>6. 4 量化NMR和弛豫反应剂</h4>\r\n<p>A. 甲.乙醇的甲基群在糖和芳香信号区域产生共,因此可能会产生错误的整合.</p>\r\n<p>B. 0.1 ml 99.9% D2O 可以含有 0.1 % HDO/H2O,这相当于 0.0001 ml. 在 0.7 ml 的 Grappa 中,有 0.44 ml H2O,因此可能的错误将是 0.023%.</p>\r\n<p>C. 由于对磁性弛豫,Cr ((acac) 3引入了线路扩展,这影响了信号强度.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-299":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>D. .重叠信号来自C-2和C-3.如果在这些碳原子周围画圆,则只能在第三球体中看到相当微小的环境差异.Cr(acac) 3的线宽展宽导致这些信号重叠.</p>\r\n<h4>6.5 缔合常数 <i>K</i><sub>a</sub> 的测定</h4><p>A. 紫外与荧光光谱。</p>\r\n<p>B. 扩散常数比基于频率的化学位移不太可靠.</p>\r\n<h4>6.6 STD NMR</h4>\r\n<p>A. 在相同的条件下,但没有任何蛋白质的相同样本进行测量.</p>\r\n<p>B. 许多蛋白质在低于TMS频率的频率上吸收.否则,如果质没有香水信号,则可以辐射蛋白质的芳香残留物.</p>\r\n<p>C. 通过延长辐射时间,获得积累曲线 (类似于NOE积累曲线).</p>\r\n<h4>6.7 动力学实验</h4>\r\n<p>A. Michaelis–Menten 方程为 v = v max [S]/( K M + [S]) 。边界条件为稳态条件、[底物] ≫ [酶]，以及酶—底物复合物向酶—产物复合物的不可逆反应。</p>\r\n<p>B. 在下一次运行中激动之前,所有信号都必须弛豫,否则动力学将会错误.</p>\r\n<p>C. 两维文件确保所有光谱都运行相同. 处理过程,例如相位校正,将是相同的.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-300":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>第 7 章答案</h3><h4>7.1 CP/MAS 实验</h4>\r\n<p>A. 在快速重复脉冲下观察 79 Br 的 FID。由于两者的 NMR 频率很接近，这可以在不失谐的 13 C 通道上完成（例如在 600 MHz 谱仪上， 79 Br 频率仅比 13 C 低 540 kHz）。FID 会受到尖锐旋转边带调制；调节魔角螺钉，直到这些边带贯穿整个 FID。</p>\r\n<p>B. 为建立 Hartmann–Hahn 条件，质子与 X 核（如 13 C）上的射频场必须相等，也就是 90° 脉冲宽度必须相等。以 4 kHz 转速检测金刚烷的质子信号，把功率调到质子 90° 脉冲为 5–6 μs；再在该功率下以去耦方式观察金刚烷的 13 C 信号，并相应设定 13 C 功率。这些功率可作为 Hartmann–Hahn 调节的起始值。保持质子功率不变，改变 13 C 功率，直到信号最大。</p>\r\n<p>C. CO 基团具有大得多的化学位移各向异性。</p>\r\n<h4>7.2 高分辨魔角旋转</h4>\r\n<p>A. 以恒定角速度做圆周运动的质点，其向心加速度为 a = ω 2 × r 。取 r = 2 × 10 −3 m、ω = 2π f 、 f = 8 kHz，可得 ω 2 = 2.53 × 10 9 s −2 ， a = 5.05 × 10 6 m s −2 。</p>\r\n<p>与地球重力加速度（<i>g</i> = 9.81 m s<sup>−2</sup>）相比，约为五十万倍。考虑 0.5 mm 的转子壁厚后，该比值降至约 390000。</p>\r\n<p>B. 转子外壁与空气摩擦会产生热耗散。</p>\r\n<p>C. 魔角旋转会调制两个核之间的偶极相互作用，因此自弛豫行为可能随转速改变；样品温度也可能变化（参见 B）。此外，巨大的 g 力（参见 A）还可能导致分子构象改变。</p>\r\n<h4>7.3 HN HSQC</h4>\r\n<p>A. 演化期结束后，同时存在 2 I Hz I Nx 与 2 I Hz I Ny 。相位为 x 的 p15 和 p6 只把后者转换为 2 I Hy I Nz ；它在 [d3, p16, p7, d3] 期间演化为 I Hx ，随后由 p8 储存为 I Hz 。同时，p15 和 p6 把 2 I Hz I Nx 转换为双量子磁化 2 I Hy I Nx ；相位为 y 的 p18 和 p9 再将其转换为 2 I Hy I Nz 。它在 [d3, p18, p9, d3] 期间演化为 I Hx 。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-301":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>最后一个 90° 脉冲 p10 不改变这一分量，但把储存的磁化 <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>Hz</sub></span> 转换为 <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>Hy</sub></span>，因此两个初始分量都得到保留。</p>\r\n<p>B. γ H 与 γ N 之比约为 10:1。在梯度 g2 处，相干路径中只有 I N + 或 I N − ；在 g5 处只有 I N − 。为满足 (g2 × I N + ) + (g5 × I N − ) = 0，梯度之比也必须为 10:1。</p>\r\n<p>C. 很难。NH 质子与 13 C 的两键和三键自旋耦合量级与这些质子的线宽相当。不过，确实存在能够完成该任务的脉冲序列。</p>\r\n<h4>7.4 HNCA</h4>\r\n<p>A. 在传统演化期中， t 1 或 t 2 会随演化逐步增加。由于弛豫依赖时间，演化期初与末端产生的信号权重不同；自旋耦合贡献也有同样问题。在恒时演化方案中，180° 脉冲在整个演化期内移动，因此所有信号权重相同，但也承受相同的弛豫损失。恒时演化期更难编程和设置。</p>\r\n<p>B. 这一效应可由同核去耦理解。照射一个质子会使邻近质子的共振位置移向更高频率；附加去耦场使旋转坐标系中的有效 B 0 场大于无去耦时的值。</p>\r\n<p>C.</p>\r\n<div class=\"appendix-table-wrap\"><table class=\"appendix-table\"><thead><tr><th>化学位移</th><th>归属</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8.66 / 62.4 and 56.3</td><td><code>T12 and K11</code></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td>8.52 / 57.9 and 53.6</td><td><code>K63 and Q62</code></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td>8.19 / 56.6 and 55.9</td><td><code>E51 and L50</code></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td>7.87 / 59.8 and 55.4</td><td><code>K29 and A28</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-302":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h3>第 8 章答案</h3><h4>8.1 发射通道脉冲宽度的校准</h4>\r\n<p>A. 这类探头具有最高的质子灵敏度。为此，其谐振电路采用较高的品质因数，以获得较高的感应响应电压；缺点是强信号存在时可能出现辐射阻尼。</p>\r\n<p>B. 接近 360° 的脉冲几乎不产生横向磁化，而会使磁化返回 +z，因此两次实验之间无需因 T 1 弛豫而等待很长时间。此外，从图 8.1-3 可见，360° 脉冲的零点比 180° 脉冲的零点更可靠。</p>\r\n<p>C. 生物缓冲液含有多种无机盐，会显著影响磁化率，并可能因离子存在而在样品中诱发涡流。</p>\r\n<h4>8.2 间接通道脉冲宽度的校准</h4>\r\n<p>A. 该谱本质上属于高阶谱，应视为 A 2 、A 2 ′、X、X′ 自旋体系；参见 P. Stilbs、S. Forsén：“Spin-spin couplings in doubly 15 N labeled urea and 1,3-dimethylurea”， Org. Magn. Reson. 1976, 8, 384–386。</p>\r\n<p>B. 结果与不施加去耦脉冲时相同：反相双峰保持不变。 C.</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-302-indirect-channel-sequence\"><img alt=\"间接通道脉冲校准的脉冲序列与相干转移路径。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/pulse-sequences/pdf-302-indirect-channel-sequence.webp\"/></figure>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-303":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<p>若 p2 = 0，则只适用虚线路径。在第一个梯度 g1 处存在 <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup><i>I</i><sub>C</sub><sup>+</sup></span>，其相干级为 −4 + 1 = −3；进入接收器的 <span class=\"inline-equation\"><i>I</i><sub>H</sub><sup>−</sup></span> 相干级为 −4。因此 g1 与 g2 必须取 4:(−3)，才能满足 (g1 × 相干级 1) + (g2 × 相干级 2) = 0。</p>\r\n<p>若 p2 = 90°，则适用实线路径，并且没有单量子相干进入接收器。</p>\r\n<h4>8.3 成形脉冲</h4>\r\n<p>A. “跃迁”选择性：1–2 Hz，用于 SPT 实验。“多重峰”选择性：50 Hz，用于选择性 NOE 或 COSY 实验。“频带”选择性：30 ppm，用于三维蛋白质 NMR，以及对全部 C=O 或全部 C α 碳原子的选择性脉冲。</p>\r\n<p>B. 1/[4 × 10 × 10 −6 s] = 25 kHz，即适用于 400 MHz 谱仪；1/[4 × 2 × 10 −3 s] = 125 Hz，即足以实现完全水峰抑制。</p>\r\n<p>C. 若 13 C 信号与 1 H 耦合，其带宽为 3 × 130 Hz = 390 Hz；否则 10 Hz 应已足够。</p>\r\n<h4>8.4 绝热脉冲</h4>\r\n<p>A. 引自维基百科（2012 年 8 月，http://en.wikipedia.org）：“若系统容器具有隔热壁，或过程在极短时间内发生，以至没有显著热交换的机会，就可能出现绝热过程。‘adiabatic’字面意为不可通过，源自希腊语词根 α-（‘不’）、δια-（‘穿过’）和 βαίνειν（‘通过’）；这里的词源含义对应于没有热传递。”</p>\r\n<p>B. 复合脉冲；这类脉冲目前仍用于去耦和自旋锁。</p>\r\n<h4>8.5 NMR 温度校准</h4>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-303-equation-2\"><img alt=\"340 K 与 345 K 的温度校准计算式。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/equations/pdf-303-equation-2.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>误差量级约为 1 kJ/mol。</p>\r\n<p>B. 样品管与深度冷却射频线圈之间所需的隔热会限制温度范围。因此，低温探头主要针对生物样品开发，因为水在 273 K 会冻结。</p>\r\n<p>C. 有机化学中最常用的是 Seyferth 混合物，即均为氘代形式的 THF 与二甲醚。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n","pdf-304":"<div class=\"source-page-layout source-sidebar-right\">\r\n<div class=\"source-main-column\">\r\n<h4>8.6 脉冲场梯度的校准</h4><p>A.</p>\r\n<figure class=\"figure source-figure appendix-facsimile\" data-figure-id=\"pdf-304-gradient-calibration-sequence\"><img alt=\"脉冲场梯度校准所用的脉冲序列。\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../assets/pulse-sequences/pdf-304-gradient-calibration-sequence.webp\"/></figure>\r\n<p>B. 可以。它给出相同结果；即使发射频率偏置不在共振位置也能工作，因为 180° 脉冲会使化学位移重新聚焦。</p>\r\n<p>C. 演化期通常远长于梯度脉冲，因此梯度脉冲并不一定需要很短的振铃衰减时间。</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<aside aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"source-sidebar source-sidebar-empty\"></aside>\r\n</div>\r\n"},"figurePlacements":{}});
